{ % vim: set ft=djot : % }

# Memory measurement

These opt-in developer probes complement the [test workflow](../CONTRIBUTING.dj#Test). The snapshot design and its memory tradeoff
are documented beside `Document` in [the analysis
module](../src/analysis/mod.rs).

## Process memory probe

On Linux, run the opt-in process memory probe with:

``` sh
cargo test --release --test lsp_test stdio_memory_probe -- --ignored --exact --nocapture
```

It starts fresh stdio servers for 100, 500 and 1000 generated documents,
with prose, inline markup, headings, links and footnotes. Each run
reports total source size, resident memory (RSS) and peak RSS after
indexing, queries without edits, opening buffers, repeated edits and
queries, saving, closing all buffers, and removing the corpus. Four
buffers are edited across eight rounds; rename edits are proposed
without being applied. The save phase writes the final buffer content to
disk and sends `textDocument/didSave` before measuring. Set
`DJOTLS_MEMORY_DOCUMENTS` to a positive integer to measure a different
workspace size.

The probe verifies query results and diagnostics, but does not impose a
memory threshold. RSS includes allocator retention, thread stacks and
shared pages; it does not measure live heap allocations or prove the
absence of leaks. Run in release mode and compare the same fixture on
the same machine.

The following historical measurements predate the current block-index
snapshots. They are context for the optimization, not current memory
budgets. For one x86_64 Linux release run on 2026-10-18 (Rust
`1.99.0-nightly (1ed2df61a 2026-08-04)`), the probe reported:

| Documents | Source (MiB) | Indexed RSS (MiB) | Queries only (MiB) | After edits (MiB) | Saved (MiB) | All buffers closed (MiB) |
|-----------|--------------|-------------------|--------------------|-------------------|-------------|--------------------------|
| 100       | 1.17         | 21.1              | 22.1               | 24.3              | 24.7        | 24.7                     |
| 500       | 5.86         | 74.9              | 77.9               | 80.0              | 80.4        | 80.4                     |
| 1000      | 11.71        | 142.0             | 147.5              | 150.4             | 150.6       | 150.6                    |

Empty servers used about 6 MiB. Queries increased RSS even without
unsaved changes. Saving and closing did not restore the initial RSS.
Open documents have their own analyzed snapshots, and saving refreshes
disk snapshots without discarding open buffers. The workspace index also
retains analyzed documents after buffers close. Temporary query maps,
resolver caches and response buffers can leave reusable memory in the
allocator after they are freed.

In a separate diagnostic run using a temporary glibc `mallinfo2`
preload, removing the 1000-document corpus and refreshing the index
reduced allocator allocated bytes from about 158 MiB to 2 MiB, while RSS
remained about 149 MiB. About 164 MiB was then free inside allocator
arenas. That supports allocator retention as the explanation for high
RSS after the corpus was removed; it does not establish the absence of
leaks. Allocator totals include allocation rounding and caches, and were
sampled while protocol responses were being sent.

Before switching snapshot events to a boxed slice, a separate Valgrind
Massif run drove the same workload with 100 documents (1.17 MiB source).
Peak requested heap was 13.3 MiB, plus about 3.0 MiB of allocator
overhead estimated by Massif. The `Vec<parser::Event>` backing
allocations accounted for 8.4 MiB, about 63 percent of requested heap at
that peak; this excludes the events' separately allocated annotation
strings. Stacks led through `Document::new` and primarily
`Workspace::scan`. During corpus removal and shutdown, sampled requested
heap fell to 0.64 MiB. This identifies retained parser events as a major
cost for this fixture. Massif changes allocator behavior, so its heap
measurements are separate from the uninstrumented RSS and glibc
diagnostic measurements above.

## Analysis allocation probe

To measure bytes requested by document analysis rather than process RSS,
run:

``` sh
cargo test --release --test analysis_memory_test -- --ignored --exact analysis_memory_probe --nocapture --test-threads=1
```

This separate test binary wraps Rust's system allocator with
allocation-free counters. It measures retained requested bytes and peak
requested bytes during `Document::new`, including source text, temporary
event storage, annotation strings and analysis tables. Retained bytes
exclude the released event stream. The probe also measures temporary
completion allocations and checks that each query returns to its
retained-allocation baseline. Inputs and warmed, shared parser regex
caches are outside the baseline. Allocator padding, freed arenas, LSP
runtime and Git indexing metadata are excluded. Peak accounting does not
include internal implementation details of the underlying allocator's
`realloc`.

Before switching snapshot events to a boxed slice, one x86_64 release
run produced the following results. File measurements refer to their
content at the time of this run, before adding this section.

| Input                        | Source    | Retained   | Peak       | Retained/source |
|------------------------------|-----------|------------|------------|-----------------|
| README.dj                    | 1.40 KiB  | 20.3 KiB   | 29.0 KiB   | 14.6            |
| docs/lsp.dj                  | 21.6 KiB  | 116.2 KiB  | 143.6 KiB  | 5.4             |
| Plain prose                  | 1.00 MiB  | 3.92 MiB   | 4.71 MiB   | 3.9             |
| Mixed markup                 | 1.00 MiB  | 7.92 MiB   | 10.14 MiB  | 7.9             |
| Dense short markup           | 1.00 MiB  | 46.26 MiB  | 85.01 MiB  | 46.3            |
| 1000 process-probe documents | 11.71 MiB | 117.35 MiB | 117.38 MiB | 10.0            |

Plain prose repeats ordinary paragraphs. Mixed markup uses distinct
headings, paragraphs, emphasis, code, Unicode and external links. Dense
markup repeats ``*a* _b_ `c` [d](https://example.com)`` with spaces; it
is a synthetic high-event-density case. The corpus measurement keeps all
1000 analyses alive at once. Each document/corpus dropped back to its
allocator baseline; the test checks this explicitly.

An `Event` occupies 40 bytes on this platform: two byte offsets and an
owned string header. The 1 MiB mixed sample had 115584 events, capacity
131072, 5 MiB of vector storage (including 0.59 MiB unused capacity),
and 0.65 MiB of separately allocated annotation strings. The dense
sample had 538461 events and capacity 1048576: vector storage alone was
40 MiB, including 19.46 MiB unused capacity. Document size and syntax
density both affect memory, and vector capacity growth creates
discontinuities rather than a fixed ratio.

The generated fixtures were measured across three snapshot designs. The
vector and boxed-event columns are historical; current snapshots retain
a block index and release the parser events after analysis.

| Input                        | Source    | Vector events | Boxed events | Block index |
|------------------------------|-----------|---------------|--------------|-------------|
| Plain prose                  | 1.00 MiB  | 3.92 MiB      | 2.69 MiB     | 1.31 MiB    |
| Mixed markup                 | 1.00 MiB  | 7.92 MiB      | 7.33 MiB     | 2.30 MiB    |
| Dense short markup           | 1.00 MiB  | 46.26 MiB     | 26.80 MiB    | 3.26 MiB    |
| 1000 process-probe documents | 11.71 MiB | 117.35 MiB    | 91.67 MiB    | 31.70 MiB   |

Boxing removed spare event capacity but retained annotation strings. The
block index removes both from long-lived snapshots. Construction still
uses a temporary event vector, so lower retained memory does not imply a
lower construction peak. These are Rust-requested bytes, excluding
allocator padding and RSS. File-based measurements above describe the
files at the time of the historical run; rerun the probe for their
current contents.

These measurements include source text and all retained analysis
records, not just parsing boundaries. All counted allocations returned
to baseline after document drop, and completion queries retained no
additional allocations. The mixed 1 MiB fixture needed about 13.8 KiB of
additional requested bytes at completion time. The dense fixture is one
enormous paragraph, so a query still parses that entire paragraph and
temporarily requests about 84.0 MiB. Construction peak for that fixture
remained 85.01 MiB. Local parsing reduces long-lived storage; it does
not bound the cost of a single enormous block.

Regression tests compare local completion against the full-document
event implementation at every byte offset in upstream syntax fixtures,
formatter inputs and incomplete inputs. Additional cases exercise block
attributes, nested containers, code and math, adjacent blocks and CRLF
line endings.
